export const V115_H = [
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 22. Docker, Kubernetes and cloud platforms
  Q('nq-302', DK, 'E', 'scenario', 'Have you worked with Docker and Kubernetes?',
    [kp('Concrete Docker usage: Dockerfiles, multi-stage builds, compose', 'dockerfile', 'multi stage', 'multi-stage', 'compose'), kp('Kubernetes objects used: Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps, Secrets, Ingress, HPA', 'deployment*', 'service*', 'configmap*', 'ingress', 'hpa'), kp('Helm, managed clusters (EKS/AKS/GKE)', 'helm', 'eks', 'aks', 'gke'), kp('Troubleshooting with kubectl', 'kubectl', 'logs', 'describe'), kp('Honest scope of experience', 'honest', 'my role', 'responsib*')],
    'Give a concrete, honest answer. For example: I containerised our Spring Boot services with multi-stage Dockerfiles, using a JDK image to build and a slim JRE image to run, running as a non-root user, and used Docker Compose locally to start PostgreSQL, Kafka and Redis. On Kubernetes, our services ran on AKS, or EKS, deployed with Helm charts: Deployments with liveness and readiness probes on Actuator, Services and an Ingress, ConfigMaps and Secrets for configuration, resource requests and limits, and HorizontalPodAutoscaler. I troubleshoot with kubectl get, describe, logs and exec, and look at events for CrashLoopBackOff or OOMKilled. Clarify what the platform team owned and what you did yourself.',
    'What was the hardest Kubernetes issue you debugged?', sh),
  Q('nq-303', DK, 'E', 'concept', 'What is Docker used for?',
    [kp('Packages an application with its dependencies into an image', 'package*', 'image', 'dependencies'), kp('Runs isolated containers sharing the host kernel', 'container*', 'isolat*', 'kernel'), kp('Consistent environments: works the same everywhere', 'consistent', 'works on my machine', 'everywhere', 'same'), kp('Lightweight compared to VMs; fast startup', 'lightweight', 'virtual machine*', 'vm', 'fast'), kp('Basis for CI/CD and orchestration', 'ci/cd', 'kubernetes', 'orchestrat*')],
    'Docker packages an application together with its runtime, libraries and configuration into an image, and runs it as an isolated container. Containers share the host operating system kernel but have their own filesystem, processes and network through namespaces and cgroups, so they are much lighter and faster to start than virtual machines. The main benefit is consistency: the same image runs identically on a laptop, in CI and in production, which removes it works on my machine problems. It is used to run services and their dependencies locally, to build reproducible CI pipelines, and as the unit of deployment for orchestrators like Kubernetes.',
    'What is the difference between an image and a container?', sh),
  Q('nq-304', K8, 'E', 'concept', 'What is Kubernetes used for?',
    [kp('Container orchestration across a cluster', 'orchestrat*', 'cluster'), kp('Scheduling, scaling, self-healing (restarts, rescheduling)', 'schedul*', 'scal*', 'self heal*', 'self-heal*', 'restart*'), kp('Service discovery and load balancing', 'service discovery', 'load balanc*', 'service'), kp('Rolling updates and rollbacks', 'rolling update*', 'rollback'), kp('Declarative desired state; config and secrets', 'declarative', 'desired state', 'configmap*', 'secret*')],
    'Kubernetes is an orchestration platform that runs containers across a cluster of machines. You declare the desired state in YAML, for example run three replicas of this image with these resources, and Kubernetes continuously makes reality match it: it schedules pods onto nodes with free capacity, restarts crashed containers, replaces pods when a node dies, scales replicas manually or automatically with the HorizontalPodAutoscaler, gives services stable DNS names with load balancing, performs rolling updates and rollbacks, and injects configuration and secrets. It turns a fleet of servers into one platform for running microservices reliably.',
    'What are the main components of the Kubernetes control plane?', sh),
  Q('nq-305', DK, 'E', 'scenario', 'How can you check application logs in a containerized environment?',
    [kp('docker logs / kubectl logs (with -f, --previous, -c container)', 'docker logs', 'kubectl logs', '--previous', '-f'), kp('Apps log to stdout/stderr', 'stdout', 'stderr', 'standard output'), kp('kubectl describe / events for crashes', 'describe', 'events'), kp('Centralised logging: ELK/EFK, Loki, CloudWatch, Azure Monitor', 'elk', 'efk', 'loki', 'cloudwatch', 'azure monitor', 'splunk'), kp('Structured JSON logs with correlation ids', 'json', 'structured', 'correlation id*', 'trace id')],
    'Containers should log to standard output and standard error. For a single container, docker logs -f name shows them. In Kubernetes, kubectl logs pod-name, with -f to follow, -c for a specific container in a multi-container pod, --previous to see the logs of the crashed previous instance, and -l app=orders for all pods with a label. kubectl describe pod and kubectl get events explain restarts, OOMKilled or probe failures. Since pods are ephemeral, production relies on centralised logging: an agent such as Fluent Bit ships logs to Elasticsearch with Kibana, Loki with Grafana, CloudWatch or Azure Monitor, where you search across pods. Structured JSON logs with trace and correlation ids make that search effective.',
    'How do you see logs of a container that keeps crashing?', sh),
  Q('nq-306', K8, 'M', 'concept', 'How does Kubernetes support application scalability?',
    [kp('Deployments with replica count; kubectl scale', 'replica*', 'kubectl scale'), kp('HorizontalPodAutoscaler on CPU/memory/custom metrics', 'horizontalpodautoscaler', 'hpa', 'cpu', 'custom metric*'), kp('Cluster Autoscaler / Karpenter adds nodes', 'cluster autoscaler', 'karpenter', 'nodes'), kp('Services load-balance across replicas; VPA, KEDA for event-driven', 'load balanc*', 'vpa', 'keda'), kp('Requires stateless apps and correct resource requests', 'stateless', 'resource request*')],
    'Applications run as Deployments with a number of replicas, and a Service load-balances traffic across them, so scaling out means more pods: kubectl scale or changing replicas in Git. The HorizontalPodAutoscaler does this automatically based on CPU, memory or custom metrics like requests per second or Kafka lag, and KEDA scales on event sources, even to zero. The Vertical Pod Autoscaler adjusts resource requests. When pods cannot be scheduled because nodes are full, the Cluster Autoscaler or Karpenter adds nodes and removes idle ones. For this to work, applications must be stateless, start quickly, have correct resource requests and readiness probes.',
    'Why do accurate resource requests matter for autoscaling?', sh),
  Q('nq-307', AWS, 'E', 'scenario', 'What cloud platforms have you worked with?',
    [kp('Name platforms: AWS, Azure, GCP', 'aws', 'azure', 'gcp', 'google cloud'), kp('Services used: compute, storage, databases, messaging', 'ec2', 'eks', 'aks', 's3', 'rds', 'sqs', 'lambda'), kp('How you deployed/managed: IaC, CI/CD', 'terraform', 'cloudformation', 'bicep', 'ci/cd'), kp('Security/monitoring: IAM, CloudWatch/Azure Monitor', 'iam', 'cloudwatch', 'azure monitor'), kp('Certifications or depth, honestly', 'certif*', 'honest')],
    'Name the platforms and the services you actually used, with context. For example: on AWS our Spring Boot services ran on EKS, images in ECR, data in RDS PostgreSQL and S3, asynchronous processing with SQS and Lambda, secrets in Secrets Manager, monitoring in CloudWatch, with infrastructure in Terraform. On Azure I worked with AKS, Azure Container Registry, Azure Service Bus, Blob Storage, Key Vault and Application Insights, deployed through Azure DevOps pipelines. Mention IAM roles and networking you configured, certifications if any, and be clear about which parts a platform team handled.',
    'How did you manage secrets in that cloud setup?', sh),
  Q('nq-308', AWS, 'M', 'concept', 'What are the equivalents of AWS Lambda and Azure Functions?',
    [kp('AWS Lambda equals Azure Functions (serverless functions)', 'azure functions', 'lambda', 'serverless'), kp('Google Cloud Functions / Cloud Run functions', 'google cloud functions', 'cloud run', 'gcp'), kp('Other: Cloudflare Workers, OpenFaaS/Knative', 'cloudflare', 'knative', 'openfaas'), kp('Event-driven, pay-per-execution, auto-scaling', 'event driven', 'event-driven', 'pay per', 'auto scal*')],
    'AWS Lambda and Azure Functions are equivalents of each other: both are function-as-a-service offerings where you deploy a function triggered by events such as HTTP requests, queue messages, file uploads or timers, the platform scales it automatically, and you pay per execution and duration. Google Cloud has Cloud Functions, now Cloud Run functions, and also Cloud Run for containers. Other options are Cloudflare Workers at the edge, and on Kubernetes, Knative or OpenFaaS. For Java, Spring Cloud Function lets the same code run on any of these.',
    'What are cold starts, and how do you reduce them for Java functions?', sh),
  Q('nq-309', AWS, 'E', 'concept', 'What is the difference between Amazon S3 and Azure Blob Storage?',
    [kp('Both are object storage services, equivalent', 'object storage', 'equivalent', 'similar'), kp('S3: buckets and objects; Blob: storage accounts, containers, blobs', 'bucket*', 'storage account*', 'container*', 'blob*'), kp('Storage tiers: S3 Standard/IA/Glacier vs Hot/Cool/Archive', 'glacier', 'hot', 'cool', 'archive', 'tier*'), kp('Access control: IAM/bucket policies, presigned URLs vs RBAC/SAS tokens', 'iam', 'bucket polic*', 'presigned', 'sas', 'rbac'), kp('Blob types: block, append, page', 'block blob', 'append', 'page blob')],
    'They are equivalent object storage services for files such as documents, images, backups and data lakes, both highly durable and accessed over HTTP APIs. The structure differs: S3 has buckets containing objects identified by keys; Azure has storage accounts containing containers containing blobs, with block, append and page blob types. Both offer storage tiers to save cost: S3 Standard, Infrequent Access and Glacier, versus Azure Hot, Cool, Cold and Archive, with lifecycle rules to move data. Security uses IAM policies, bucket policies and presigned URLs in AWS, and Azure RBAC with Entra ID and SAS tokens in Azure. Both support versioning, encryption and event notifications.',
    'How would you let a browser upload a file directly without passing through your API?', sh),
  Q('nq-310', K8, 'M', 'concept', 'What is Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS)?',
    [kp('Managed Kubernetes on Azure: Azure runs the control plane', 'managed', 'control plane'), kp('You manage node pools (VM scale sets)', 'node pool*', 'scale set*'), kp('Integrations: Entra ID, ACR, Azure Monitor, Key Vault, networking (CNI)', 'entra', 'azure ad', 'acr', 'azure monitor', 'key vault', 'cni'), kp('Autoscaling, upgrades, workload identity', 'autoscal*', 'upgrade*', 'workload identity'), kp('Equivalent to EKS/GKE', 'eks', 'gke')],
    'AKS is Azure managed Kubernetes service. Azure operates the Kubernetes control plane, API server, etcd and scheduler, for free or with an SLA tier, while you manage node pools, which are VM scale sets running your pods, choosing VM sizes, system and user pools, and autoscaling. It integrates with Azure services: Microsoft Entra ID for cluster authentication and RBAC, Azure Container Registry for images, Azure CNI networking with VNets, Application Gateway or NGINX ingress, Azure Monitor Container Insights, Key Vault through the Secrets Store CSI driver, and workload identity so pods access Azure resources without secrets. Upgrades and node image patching are simplified. It is the Azure equivalent of EKS and GKE.',
    'How would a pod securely access Azure Key Vault?', sh),
  Q('nq-311', AWS, 'E', 'concept', 'What is Amazon SQS?',
    [kp('Fully managed message queue service', 'managed', 'message queue', 'queue'), kp('Standard (at-least-once, best-effort order) vs FIFO (exactly-once processing, ordered)', 'standard', 'fifo', 'at least once', 'at-least-once', 'order*'), kp('Decouples producers and consumers; buffers load', 'decoupl*', 'buffer*'), kp('Visibility timeout, retention, dead-letter queues', 'visibility timeout', 'retention', 'dead letter', 'dlq'), kp('Polling-based consumers; long polling', 'poll*', 'long polling')],
    'Amazon Simple Queue Service is a fully managed message queue. Producers send messages and consumers poll and delete them after processing, so the two sides are decoupled and the queue buffers bursts of load. Standard queues offer nearly unlimited throughput with at-least-once delivery and best-effort ordering, so consumers must be idempotent; FIFO queues guarantee order within a message group and exactly-once processing with deduplication, at lower throughput. Important settings are the visibility timeout, which hides a received message while it is being processed, message retention up to 14 days, delay, long polling to reduce empty responses, and a dead-letter queue for messages that fail repeatedly.',
    'When would you choose SQS over Kafka?', sh),
  Q('nq-312', AWS, 'M', 'concept', 'How does asynchronous messaging work using SQS?',
    [kp('Producer sends message and returns immediately', 'send*', 'return* immediately', 'producer'), kp('Consumer polls (ReceiveMessage), processes, then deletes', 'receivemessage', 'poll*', 'delete*'), kp('Visibility timeout hides in-flight messages; redelivery if not deleted', 'visibility timeout', 'redeliver*', 'in flight', 'in-flight'), kp('maxReceiveCount moves poison messages to DLQ', 'maxreceivecount', 'dead letter', 'dlq', 'poison'), kp('Idempotent processing; batching', 'idempot*', 'batch*')],
    'The producer calls SendMessage and immediately continues; it does not wait for processing. SQS stores the message redundantly. Consumers, such as a Spring service with @SqsListener or a Lambda, poll with ReceiveMessage, ideally long polling and in batches of up to ten. A received message is not deleted; it becomes invisible for the visibility timeout. If the consumer processes it successfully it calls DeleteMessage; if it crashes or fails, the message becomes visible again and is retried. After maxReceiveCount failed attempts, the redrive policy moves it to a dead-letter queue for investigation. Because a message can be delivered more than once, processing must be idempotent.',
    'What happens if processing takes longer than the visibility timeout?', sh),
  Q('nq-313', AWS, 'E', 'concept', 'What is AWS Lambda, and how does it process messages from a queue?',
    [kp('Serverless compute: run code on events without managing servers', 'serverless', 'without managing servers', 'event*'), kp('Event source mapping polls SQS and invokes Lambda with batches', 'event source mapping', 'batch*', 'polls'), kp('Successful batch deleted; failures returned to queue; partial batch responses', 'deleted', 'partial batch', 'batchitemfailures', 'fail*'), kp('Pay per invocation and duration; timeouts up to 15 minutes', 'pay per', '15 minutes', 'duration'), kp('DLQ and retries via redrive policy', 'dlq', 'dead letter', 'redrive')],
    'AWS Lambda runs your function code in response to events without you managing servers; you pay per request and per millisecond of execution, with a maximum run time of 15 minutes. For SQS, Lambda uses an event source mapping: the Lambda service itself long-polls the queue, collects messages into batches, by size and batching window, and invokes your function with the batch. If the function succeeds, the messages are deleted from the queue; if it throws, the whole batch becomes visible again after the visibility timeout and is retried, unless you return partial batch failures through ReportBatchItemFailures so only failed messages are retried. Repeatedly failing messages go to the dead-letter queue.',
    'Why should the queue visibility timeout be larger than the Lambda timeout?', sh),
  Q('nq-314', AWS, 'M', 'concept', 'How does Lambda scale when a queue receives many messages?',
    [kp('Event source mapping increases pollers/concurrency as the backlog grows', 'pollers', 'concurrency', 'backlog', 'scale*'), kp('Standard queues scale up quickly (up to ~1,250 concurrent with newer scaling)', '1250', '1,250', '60 per minute', 'per minute'), kp('Limited by account concurrency and reserved/maximum concurrency', 'account', 'reserved concurrency', 'maximum concurrency', 'limit*'), kp('FIFO limited by number of message groups', 'fifo', 'message group*'), kp('Throttling returns messages to queue; protect downstream', 'throttl*', 'downstream', 'database')],
    'With an SQS trigger, Lambda watches the queue backlog and adds more pollers and concurrent function instances as messages pile up, and scales back down when the queue drains. For standard queues it scales quickly, with recent improvements adding up to about three hundred concurrent executions per minute, up to around 1,250 per queue mapping, bounded by the account concurrency limit, by default 1,000 per region, and by any reserved or maximum concurrency you configure. FIFO queues scale only up to the number of active message groups, to preserve order. When Lambda is throttled, messages simply stay in or return to the queue. Fast scaling can overwhelm downstream systems like a database, so you cap concurrency.',
    'How would you stop Lambda scaling from overwhelming a relational database?', sh),
  Q('nq-315', AWS, 'M', 'concept', 'What is reserved concurrency in AWS Lambda?',
    [kp('Sets aside a number of concurrent executions for a function from the account pool', 'set aside', 'account', 'pool', 'guarantee*'), kp('Also acts as a maximum for that function', 'maximum', 'cap', 'limit*'), kp('Setting it to 0 disables/throttles the function', 'zero', 'disable*', 'throttl*'), kp('Different from provisioned concurrency (pre-warmed instances)', 'provisioned concurrency', 'pre warmed', 'cold start*'), kp('For SQS, maximum concurrency on the event source mapping is preferred', 'maximum concurrency', 'event source mapping')],
    'Reserved concurrency guarantees a function a number of concurrent executions from the regional account pool, so other functions cannot consume them, and at the same time it caps the function at that number. For example, reserving 20 means at most 20 instances run at once, which protects a downstream database or third-party API; setting it to zero effectively disables the function, a useful kill switch. It is free. It is different from provisioned concurrency, which keeps a number of instances pre-initialised to avoid cold starts and costs money. For SQS triggers, AWS added a maximum concurrency setting on the event source mapping, which limits polling without causing throttling-based retries.',
    'Why can reserved concurrency with SQS cause messages to land in the DLQ?', sh),
  Q('nq-316', AWS, 'M', 'scenario', 'How can you control the number of concurrent Lambda executions?',
    [kp('Reserved concurrency on the function', 'reserved concurrency'), kp('Maximum concurrency on the SQS event source mapping', 'maximum concurrency', 'event source mapping'), kp('Batch size and batching window', 'batch size', 'batching window'), kp('FIFO message groups limit parallelism', 'fifo', 'message group*'), kp('Account limits, API Gateway throttling, provisioned concurrency', 'account limit', 'api gateway', 'throttl*', 'provisioned')],
    'Several levers. Reserved concurrency on the function caps how many instances can run at once across all triggers. For SQS sources, set maximum concurrency on the event source mapping, which limits how many concurrent invocations the poller creates, without throttling errors. Batch size and batching window control how much work each invocation does, so fewer invocations are needed. With FIFO queues, the number of message groups limits parallelism, since each group is processed in order. For HTTP entry points, API Gateway throttling and usage plans limit request rate. And account-level concurrency quotas are a global ceiling. I choose these based on what the downstream system, for example the database connection pool, can sustain.',
    'How would you size concurrency for a database with 50 connections?', sh),
  Q('nq-317', AWS, 'M', 'concept', 'How do SQS visibility timeouts and message delays affect processing?',
    [kp('Visibility timeout: message hidden after receive; reappears if not deleted', 'hidden', 'invisible', 'reappear*', 'not deleted'), kp('Too short causes duplicate processing; too long delays retries', 'too short', 'duplicate', 'too long', 'delay* retr*', 'processed twice', 'shorter than'), kp('Set to ~6x Lambda timeout; extend with ChangeMessageVisibility', 'six times', '6x', 'changemessagevisibility'), kp('Delay queues / DelaySeconds postpone initial delivery (up to 15 min)', 'delay queue', 'delayseconds', '15 minutes'), kp('Use delays for backoff or scheduled retries', 'backoff', 'retry later', 'scheduled')],
    'The visibility timeout is how long a message stays hidden after a consumer receives it. If the consumer deletes it in time, it is gone; if not, it reappears and another consumer may process it. If the timeout is shorter than processing time, messages are processed twice in parallel; if it is very long, failed messages wait a long time before retry. AWS recommends about six times the Lambda timeout for Lambda consumers, and long-running consumers can extend it with ChangeMessageVisibility. Message delay, through a delay queue or DelaySeconds per message, up to 15 minutes, makes a new message invisible for a while before first delivery, which is useful for implementing backoff or deferring work such as checking a status later.',
    'How can you implement exponential backoff with SQS?', sh),
  Q('nq-318', AWS, 'SL', 'design', 'How would you implement a callback or polling mechanism for an asynchronous downstream system?',
    [kp('Prefer callbacks/webhooks or events from the downstream system', 'callback', 'webhook', 'event*'), kp('Correlation id stored with request state (PENDING) to match responses', 'correlation id', 'pending', 'state'), kp('If polling: scheduled/delayed checks with backoff and timeout (SQS delay, Step Functions wait)', 'polling', 'backoff', 'delay', 'step functions', 'timeout'), kp('Idempotent handling of callbacks; verify signatures', 'idempot*', 'signature', 'verify'), kp('Notify client: status endpoint, websocket, events', 'status endpoint', 'websocket', 'notify', '202')],
    'First choice is a callback: when we submit work, we send a correlation id and a callback URL or subscribe to the downstream system events, store the request as PENDING with that id, and return 202 Accepted with a status link to our own client. When the downstream system calls back, we authenticate it, verify the signature, find the request by correlation id, update state idempotently, since callbacks may be repeated, and publish an event or notify the client by webhook or WebSocket. If the downstream system cannot call back, I implement polling efficiently: a delayed SQS message or Step Functions wait state checks the status after a delay with exponential backoff, and gives up after a maximum time, marking the request as failed or escalating, instead of a tight loop.',
    'How would you handle a callback that arrives before your own record is saved?', sh),
  Q('nq-319', AWS, 'SL', 'design', 'How would you avoid repeatedly polling a database for a downstream callback?',
    [kp('Event-driven: callback handler publishes an event or message', 'event', 'publish*', 'message'), kp('Change data capture / DynamoDB Streams / database notifications', 'cdc', 'dynamodb streams', 'change data capture', 'listen/notify', 'debezium'), kp('Delayed messages or workflow wait states instead of tight loops', 'delay*', 'step functions', 'wait', 'scheduled'), kp('Push to client via websocket/SSE or webhook', 'websocket', 'server sent events', 'sse', 'webhook'), kp('If polling remains: backoff, indexes, caching', 'backoff', 'index*', 'cach*')],
    'Instead of having workers or clients query the database every few seconds for a status change, make the change produce a signal. The callback handler, after updating the record, publishes an event to SNS, SQS, Kafka or EventBridge that the waiting process consumes. Or capture changes directly from the database: DynamoDB Streams triggering a Lambda, Debezium change data capture, or PostgreSQL LISTEN and NOTIFY. Long-running workflows can wait on a Step Functions task token that the callback completes. Browsers get updates by WebSocket or server-sent events instead of polling. If some polling remains, use exponential backoff, a time limit, an indexed status query, or a cache.',
    'What is a Step Functions task token and how does it fit here?', sh),
  Q('nq-320', AWS, 'SL', 'design', 'How can a queue and Lambda be combined to orchestrate asynchronous processing?',
    [kp('API accepts request, enqueues to SQS, returns 202', 'enqueue*', 'sqs', '202', 'accept*'), kp('Lambda consumers process in batches and update state', 'lambda', 'batch*', 'state'), kp('Chain steps with multiple queues/SNS/EventBridge or Step Functions for complex flows', 'sns', 'eventbridge', 'step functions', 'chain'), kp('DLQ, retries, idempotency, concurrency limits', 'dlq', 'dead letter', 'retr*', 'idempot*', 'concurrency'), kp('Monitoring: queue depth, age of oldest message, errors', 'queue depth', 'age of oldest', 'cloudwatch', 'alarm*')],
    'A common serverless pattern: the API, API Gateway plus a Lambda or a container, validates the request, stores it with status RECEIVED and puts a message on an SQS queue, returning 202 with an id. A worker Lambda triggered by the queue processes messages in batches, calls downstream systems, updates the status and, for the next step, sends a message to the next queue or publishes an event to SNS or EventBridge, so each step scales and fails independently. For complex flows with branching, waits and compensation, Step Functions orchestrates the Lambdas instead. Reliability comes from dead-letter queues, partial batch failure reporting, idempotent handlers keyed by request id, and maximum concurrency to protect downstream systems, with CloudWatch alarms on queue depth and age of oldest message.',
    'When would you switch from chained queues to Step Functions?', sh),
  Q('nq-321', AWS, 'M', 'scenario', 'How would you handle retries and failures in an asynchronous message-processing workflow?',
    [kp('Classify errors: transient (retry) vs permanent (do not retry)', 'transient', 'permanent', 'classif*'), kp('Retries with exponential backoff and jitter, limited attempts', 'exponential backoff', 'backoff', 'jitter', 'max attempts', 'maxreceivecount'), kp('Dead-letter queue with alerting and redrive tooling', 'dead letter', 'dlq', 'alert*', 'redrive', 'replay'), kp('Idempotent consumers and deduplication', 'idempot*', 'dedup*'), kp('Compensation/state tracking and monitoring', 'compensat*', 'state', 'monitor*')],
    'I separate transient failures, such as timeouts, throttling or a temporarily unavailable service, from permanent ones, such as invalid data or business rule violations. Transient errors are retried with exponential backoff and jitter, through the queue redelivery and visibility timeout, delayed messages or a retry topic, up to a maximum number of attempts. Permanent errors are not retried; the message goes straight to a dead-letter queue or an error topic with the reason. Dead-letter queues have alarms, and tooling to inspect, fix and redrive messages. Every consumer is idempotent, deduplicating by message or business id, because retries cause duplicates. The workflow state records each step so failures can trigger compensation or manual follow-up, and dashboards track error rates and DLQ size.',
    'Why is jitter added to backoff?', sh),
  Q('nq-322', AWS, 'M', 'concept', 'What is the difference between serverless computing and managing your own infrastructure?',
    [kp('Serverless: provider manages servers, scaling, patching; you deploy code/functions', 'provider manages', 'no servers', 'patching', 'managed'), kp('Pay per use vs pay for provisioned capacity', 'pay per use', 'pay per', 'idle', 'provisioned', 'actual usage'), kp('Auto-scaling including to zero', 'scale to zero', 'auto scal*', 'automatic*'), kp('Trade-offs: cold starts, execution limits, vendor lock-in, less control', 'cold start*', 'limit*', 'lock in', 'lock-in', 'control'), kp('Own infra (VMs/K8s): full control, steady workloads cheaper, more ops effort', 'control', 'steady', 'operations', 'ops')],
    'With serverless, such as Lambda, Azure Functions, Fargate or managed databases like DynamoDB, the provider runs and patches the servers, scales automatically, even to zero, and you pay only for actual usage, so you focus on code and spiky or unpredictable workloads are cheap. The trade-offs are cold starts, execution time and memory limits, less control over the runtime and networking, harder local testing, and stronger vendor lock-in. Managing your own infrastructure, VMs or Kubernetes clusters, gives full control over runtime, tuning and networking, predictable performance, and is often cheaper for steady high-volume workloads, but your team must handle capacity planning, scaling, patching, security and on-call operations.',
    'Which workloads in your system would you move to serverless?', sh),
  Q('nq-323', K8, 'M', 'scenario', 'What experience do you have with OpenShift and Cloud Platform Foundation?',
    [kp('OpenShift: Red Hat enterprise Kubernetes distribution', 'openshift', 'red hat', 'kubernetes'), kp('OpenShift features: Routes, projects, SCCs, BuildConfigs/S2I, oc CLI, operators', 'route*', 'project*', 'scc', 'security context constraint*', 'source to image', 's2i', 'oc '), kp('Cloud Foundry: PaaS with cf push, buildpacks, services marketplace', 'cloud foundry', 'cf push', 'buildpack*', 'pcf', 'tanzu'), kp('Describe your actual usage honestly', 'used', 'deployed', 'honest', 'project')],
    'Answer from experience and be honest about depth. OpenShift is Red Hat enterprise Kubernetes distribution: it adds Projects on top of namespaces, Routes for external traffic, stricter security by default through Security Context Constraints, so containers run as random non-root users, built-in image builds with BuildConfigs and Source-to-Image, an integrated registry, a web console, the oc CLI and OperatorHub. A typical answer: we deployed Spring Boot services to OpenShift with Helm or templates and had to adjust images to run as non-root. Cloud Foundry, sold as Pivotal Cloud Foundry and VMware Tanzu, which interviewers sometimes call Cloud Platform Foundation, is a platform as a service where cf push uploads your jar, buildpacks build it, and you bind marketplace services like databases, which is simpler but less flexible than Kubernetes.',
    'What changes did you need to run a container on OpenShift?', sh),

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 23. Agile, Scrum and technical leadership
  Q('nq-324', AG, 'E', 'scenario', 'What is your experience working with Agile and Scrum?',
    [kp('Team setup and sprint length', 'sprint*', 'two week*', 'team'), kp('Ceremonies you took part in', 'planning', 'stand up', 'stand-up', 'review', 'retrospective'), kp('Roles: product owner, scrum master, developers', 'product owner', 'scrum master'), kp('Tools: Jira, boards, definition of done', 'jira', 'board', 'definition of done'), kp('Example of improving the process', 'improv*', 'example', 'velocity', 'retro')],
    'Describe the real setup and your part in it. For example: I worked in a Scrum team of six developers and two QA with a product owner and a scrum master, in two-week sprints. We held sprint planning, daily stand-ups, backlog refinement, sprint reviews with stakeholders and retrospectives, and tracked work in Jira with a definition of ready and definition of done that included code review, tests and deployment to QA. As a senior developer I helped break features into stories, estimated with story points, and took actions from retros, for example we reduced carry-over by splitting stories smaller and limiting work in progress. Mention Kanban too if you used it for support work.',
    'What is one thing you would change in how your last team ran Scrum?', sh),
  Q('nq-325', AG, 'E', 'concept', 'What is the difference between Agile and Waterfall?',
    [kp('Waterfall: sequential phases, requirements fixed upfront', 'sequential', 'phases', 'upfront', 'fixed'), kp('Agile: iterative, incremental delivery in short cycles', 'iterative', 'incremental', 'short cycle*', 'sprint*'), kp('Change handling: costly vs welcomed', 'change*', 'adapt*'), kp('Feedback early and continuously vs at the end', 'feedback', 'early', 'end'), kp('When each fits', 'fits', 'regulated', 'fixed scope', 'when', 'can still suit', 'suit')],
    'Waterfall runs a project in sequential phases: requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, each completed and signed off before the next, with scope fixed upfront and working software appearing only at the end, so late changes are expensive and problems are found late. Agile delivers in short iterations, sprints of one to four weeks, each producing a working, tested increment; requirements evolve through a prioritised backlog, customers give feedback every iteration, and the plan adapts. Teams are cross-functional and collaborate continuously. Waterfall can still suit projects with fixed, well-understood requirements and heavy regulation; Agile suits most software where needs change and early feedback matters.',
    'Can you combine Agile with fixed deadlines and budgets?', sh),
  Q('nq-326', AG, 'E', 'concept', 'What are the key ceremonies in Scrum?',
    [kp('Sprint planning: choose sprint goal and backlog items', 'sprint planning', 'sprint goal'), kp('Daily scrum / stand-up: 15 minutes, progress and impediments', 'daily scrum', 'stand up', 'stand-up', '15 minutes'), kp('Sprint review: demo increment to stakeholders', 'sprint review', 'demo'), kp('Sprint retrospective: improve the process', 'retrospective'), kp('Backlog refinement (ongoing activity)', 'refinement', 'grooming')],
    'Scrum defines the sprint itself as a container for four events. Sprint planning, at the start, where the team and product owner agree a sprint goal and select and plan backlog items. The daily scrum, a 15-minute stand-up where developers inspect progress toward the goal and raise impediments. The sprint review at the end, where the team demonstrates the increment to stakeholders and the backlog is adapted from their feedback. And the sprint retrospective, where the team reflects on how they worked and picks improvements. Backlog refinement is not formally an event but an ongoing activity to clarify and estimate upcoming items.',
    'What makes a daily stand-up ineffective?', sh),
  Q('nq-327', AG, 'E', 'concept', 'What happens during daily stand-ups and sprint retrospectives?',
    [kp('Stand-up: short sync on progress towards sprint goal, plans and blockers', 'progress', 'blocker*', 'impediment*', 'sprint goal'), kp('Three questions or walking the board', 'yesterday', 'today', 'walk the board', 'board'), kp('Details taken offline', 'offline', 'after', 'parking lot'), kp('Retro: what went well, what did not, actions', 'went well', 'improve', 'action*'), kp('Retro is safe, blameless, follows up on actions', 'blameless', 'safe', 'follow up', 'follow-up')],
    'The daily stand-up is a 15-minute sync for the developers. Each person, or the team walking the board from right to left, shares what moved toward the sprint goal, what is next, and any blockers; it is not a status report to a manager, and detailed discussions are taken offline right after with only the people involved. The scrum master helps remove impediments. The retrospective happens at the end of each sprint: the team reviews what went well, what did not and what to try, using formats like start, stop, continue, and agrees one to three concrete, owned actions, for example adding contract tests or limiting work in progress. It works only in a safe, blameless atmosphere, and actions are reviewed at the next retro.',
    'How do you make sure retro actions are actually done?', sh),
  Q('nq-328', AG, 'E', 'scenario', 'How do you use Jira to track stories, tasks, and blockers?',
    [kp('Issue types: epics, stories, tasks/sub-tasks, bugs', 'epic*', 'stor*', 'sub task*', 'sub-task*', 'bug*'), kp('Board columns/workflow statuses; sprint board', 'board', 'workflow', 'status*', 'column*'), kp('Blockers: flags, blocked status, issue links', 'flag*', 'blocked', 'link*'), kp('Estimates, acceptance criteria, linking to commits/PRs', 'story point*', 'acceptance criteria', 'commit*', 'pull request'), kp('Reports: burndown, velocity, dashboards', 'burndown', 'velocity', 'dashboard*')],
    'We organise work as epics for larger features, broken into user stories with acceptance criteria and story points, and stories into sub-tasks or tasks for technical work, plus bugs. The sprint board has workflow columns such as To Do, In Progress, Code Review, QA and Done, and I keep my items updated so the board reflects reality. Blockers are made visible with the flag or a Blocked status, a comment explaining what is needed and from whom, and an is blocked by link to the blocking issue, often in another team, and we raise them in stand-up. Commits and pull requests reference the issue key, so Jira shows development status. We use burndown charts, velocity and dashboards in planning and reviews.',
    'How do you track a dependency on another team in Jira?', sh),
  Q('nq-329', AG, 'M', 'scenario', 'How do you prioritize tasks across a team?',
    [kp('Align with business value and goals (product owner, sprint goal)', 'business value', 'product owner', 'sprint goal', 'goal*'), kp('Frameworks: MoSCoW, value vs effort, WSJF, urgency/impact', 'moscow', 'value vs effort', 'wsjf', 'impact', 'urgen*'), kp('Dependencies, risk and deadlines', 'dependenc*', 'risk', 'deadline*'), kp('Balance features, tech debt, bugs and production issues', 'tech debt', 'technical debt', 'bug*', 'production'), kp('Transparent communication and re-prioritisation', 'transparen*', 'communicat*', 're-priorit*', 'stakeholder*')],
    'Priorities start from business value and the goals agreed with the product owner, so the backlog order is the main input. Within that I weigh urgency and impact, production incidents and security issues first, deadlines and dependencies, doing the work that unblocks other teams or reduces the biggest risk early, and effort, using value versus effort or WSJF for larger items, and MoSCoW for releases. I reserve some capacity, around fifteen to twenty percent, for technical debt and bugs so they do not pile up. Then I assign work by skills and growth, avoid having everyone on many things at once, and when new urgent work arrives, I make the trade-off explicit with the product owner and stakeholders.',
    'How do you handle two stakeholders who both say their request is top priority?', sh),
  Q('nq-330', AG, 'M', 'concept', 'What is quarterly planning?',
    [kp('Planning work for the next quarter around goals/OKRs', 'quarter*', 'okr*', 'goals'), kp('Roadmap and epics; capacity planning', 'roadmap', 'epic*', 'capacity'), kp('Cross-team dependencies and risks identified (SAFe PI planning)', 'dependenc*', 'pi planning', 'safe', 'risk*'), kp('Commitments and stretch goals, revisited regularly', 'commit*', 'stretch', 'review*', 'adjust*')],
    'Quarterly planning is a longer-horizon planning session, typically every three months, where teams align their work with company objectives, often expressed as OKRs. Product and engineering agree which epics and initiatives will be tackled, size them roughly, for example with t-shirt sizes, compare with team capacity after holidays, support work and tech debt, and identify cross-team dependencies and risks. In scaled frameworks like SAFe this is called PI planning, program increment planning, with all teams together. The result is a roadmap with committed objectives and stretch goals; sprints then break it down, and the plan is revisited when priorities change.',
    'How do you estimate work for a quarter when details are unclear?', sh),
  Q('nq-331', AG, 'E', 'concept', 'What is t-shirt sizing, and how is it used for estimation?',
    [kp('Relative estimation with sizes XS, S, M, L, XL', 'xs', 'small', 'medium', 'large', 'xl', 'relative'), kp('Used early for epics/roadmaps when details are unknown', 'early', 'epic*', 'roadmap', 'rough'), kp('Fast and avoids false precision', 'fast', 'false precision', 'quick'), kp('Sizes can map to ranges (sprints/points); large items get split', 'map*', 'range*', 'split'), kp('Team discussion uncovers assumptions', 'discussion', 'assumption*', 'consensus')],
    'T-shirt sizing is a relative estimation technique where items are sized XS, S, M, L or XL instead of hours or exact points. It is used early, for epics, roadmap items and quarterly planning, when details are unknown and precise numbers would be false precision. The team quickly compares items with each other and with known reference items, and the discussion surfaces assumptions and risks. Sizes are often mapped to rough ranges, for example M is about one sprint for the team, L two to three sprints, so planners can check capacity. Anything XL is usually too uncertain and gets split or investigated with a spike before committing.',
    'How does t-shirt sizing differ from story points?', sh),
  Q('nq-332', AG, 'E', 'concept', 'What happens during backlog refinement?',
    [kp('Product owner and team review upcoming backlog items', 'product owner', 'upcoming', 'review'), kp('Clarify requirements and acceptance criteria', 'clarif*', 'acceptance criteria', 'requirements'), kp('Split large stories, identify dependencies and risks', 'split', 'dependenc*', 'risk*'), kp('Estimate (story points) and order the backlog', 'estimat*', 'story point*', 'order*', 'priorit*'), kp('Definition of ready', 'definition of ready', 'ready')],
    'Backlog refinement, sometimes called grooming, is an ongoing session, often once a week, where the product owner and the team look at upcoming backlog items, typically the next one or two sprints worth. They clarify what the user needs and why, write or sharpen acceptance criteria, discuss technical approach and risks, identify dependencies on other teams, split stories that are too big into smaller vertical slices, add spikes for unknowns, and estimate with story points or planning poker. The goal is that items at the top of the backlog meet the definition of ready, so sprint planning is quick and the team can commit with confidence.',
    'What does "ready" mean for a user story?', sh),
  Q('nq-333', AG, 'M', 'scenario', 'How do you estimate work and agree on delivery timelines?',
    [kp('Break work into small pieces and estimate relatively (story points, planning poker)', 'break* down', 'story point*', 'planning poker', 'relative'), kp('Use historical velocity/throughput to forecast', 'velocity', 'throughput', 'historical', 'forecast*'), kp('Include testing, reviews, deployment, risks and buffer', 'testing', 'buffer', 'risk*', 'contingency'), kp('Give ranges/confidence, not single dates; spikes for unknowns', 'range*', 'confidence', 'spike*'), kp('Agree with stakeholders; scope trade-offs; update early', 'stakeholder*', 'scope', 'trade off', 'trade-off', 'early')],
    'I break the work into small stories and have the people doing it estimate relatively, with story points and planning poker, which surfaces different assumptions. Unknowns get a time-boxed spike first. I make sure estimates include testing, code review, deployment and coordination, not just coding. Then I forecast from historical velocity or throughput rather than wishful thinking, and communicate a range with confidence, for example likely in three sprints, at worst four, plus the risks. When a fixed date is required, I agree with the product owner on scope trade-offs, a minimum viable scope first. I track progress against the plan and flag deviations early rather than at the deadline.',
    'What do you do when a stakeholder pushes for a date the team thinks is unrealistic?', sh),
  Q('nq-334', AG, 'M', 'scenario', 'How do you resolve blockers and impediments for your team?',
    [kp('Make blockers visible early (stand-up, board flags)', 'visible', 'early', 'stand up', 'flag*'), kp('Understand root cause and owner', 'root cause', 'owner', 'understand'), kp('Act: unblock directly, contact other teams, escalate with options', 'escalat*', 'other team*', 'contact', 'unblock'), kp('Work around meanwhile: mocks, re-prioritise, swarm', 'workaround', 'work around', 'mock*', 're-priorit*', 'swarm*'), kp('Follow up and prevent recurrence', 'follow up', 'follow-up', 'prevent*', 'retro*')],
    'First, blockers must be visible as soon as they appear: raised in stand-up or flagged on the board, not discovered at the end of a sprint. Then I clarify what exactly is blocked, the root cause and who can resolve it. Many technical blockers I can remove myself or by pairing. For external ones, like waiting for another team API, environment access or a decision, I contact the owner directly with a specific request and deadline, and if it does not move, escalate through the scrum master or managers with options and impact. Meanwhile I keep the team productive: work against a mock or contract, reorder tasks, or swarm on other stories. Afterwards we look at why it happened in the retro to prevent it next time.',
    'Can you give an example of a blocker you escalated and how it was resolved?', sh),
  Q('nq-335', AG, 'SL', 'scenario', 'As a technical lead, how do you divide responsibilities and make technical decisions?',
    [kp('Divide by strengths, growth and ownership areas; avoid single points of knowledge', 'strength*', 'growth', 'ownership', 'knowledge', 'bus factor'), kp('Clear ownership of components/stories; pairing and mentoring', 'owner*', 'pairing', 'mentor*'), kp('Decisions: gather options, trade-offs, involve team, data/POCs', 'options', 'trade off*', 'trade-off*', 'poc', 'proof of concept', 'involve'), kp('Document decisions (ADRs) and align with architecture/stakeholders', 'adr', 'architecture decision record*', 'document*'), kp('Disagree and commit; revisit when facts change', 'disagree and commit', 'revisit', 'consensus')],
    'For responsibilities, I map the work to people based on their strengths and their growth goals, give clear ownership of components or features end to end, and deliberately rotate and pair so knowledge is not concentrated in one person. I keep myself available for unblocking, reviews and the riskiest technical parts rather than taking all interesting work. For technical decisions, I frame the problem and constraints, gather options with the team, compare trade-offs, cost, risk, operability, team skills, and when uncertain run a short proof of concept or spike. I try to reach consensus; if not, I make the call, explain the reasoning, and we disagree and commit. Significant decisions are recorded as architecture decision records and aligned with architects and stakeholders, and revisited if facts change.',
    'Can you describe a technical decision you made that turned out wrong, and what you did?', sh),

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 24. Generative AI, developer tools and MCP
  Q('nq-336', AI, 'E', 'scenario', 'How have you used Generative AI or large language models in software development?',
    [kp('Concrete uses: code generation, tests, refactoring, explaining code', 'code generation', 'test*', 'refactor*', 'explain*'), kp('Documentation, design discussions, debugging', 'documentation', 'design', 'debug*'), kp('Review and verify AI output; security/privacy care', 'review', 'verify', 'validat*', 'privacy', 'security'), kp('Measurable impact', 'faster', 'productivity', 'time', 'impact'), kp('Possibly building LLM features (APIs, RAG)', 'api', 'rag', 'retrieval', 'embedding*', 'spring ai')],
    'Give concrete examples and show judgement. For example: I use an AI assistant daily to scaffold boilerplate such as DTOs, mappers and REST controllers, to generate unit test cases including edge cases I might miss, to explain unfamiliar legacy code, to draft SQL and regular expressions, and to help debug stack traces. I also use it to draft documentation and to brainstorm design options. I always review and test the output, because it can be confidently wrong or insecure, and I do not paste secrets or customer data into tools that are not approved. If you built AI features, mention it: for example a support assistant using an LLM API with retrieval-augmented generation over our documentation, built with Spring AI. Quantify impact where you can.',
    'What risks do you watch for when using AI-generated code?', sh),
  Q('nq-337', AI, 'E', 'scenario', 'Have you worked with GitHub Copilot, Claude, or Cursor?',
    [kp('Name tools and how you used each', 'copilot', 'claude', 'cursor', 'chatgpt'), kp('Inline completion vs chat vs agentic multi-file edits', 'completion', 'chat', 'agent*', 'multi file', 'multi-file'), kp('Context: repo awareness, rules/instructions files', 'context', 'rules', 'instructions', 'codebase'), kp('Strengths and limitations you observed', 'limitation*', 'strength*', 'hallucinat*', 'wrong', 'what worked'), kp('Company policy and data privacy', 'policy', 'privacy', 'enterprise', 'approved')],
    'Answer with your real usage. For example: GitHub Copilot in IntelliJ and VS Code for inline completions and its chat for explaining code and writing tests; Claude for reasoning-heavy tasks such as reviewing a design, refactoring a large class or writing documentation, and as an agent through Claude Code to make multi-file changes and run tests; Cursor as an AI-first editor with codebase-aware chat and project rules. Explain what worked, such as boilerplate, tests and migrations, and what did not, such as subtle concurrency bugs or invented APIs, so you always review diffs and run tests. Mention that you used the enterprise versions approved by your company, which do not train on your code.',
    'How do you give these tools enough context about your codebase?', sh),
  Q('nq-338', AI, 'M', 'scenario', 'How have you used AI tools to speed up application development?',
    [kp('Scaffolding and boilerplate generation', 'scaffold*', 'boilerplate', 'generat*'), kp('Writing tests and test data', 'test*', 'test data'), kp('Refactoring/migrations (e.g. Java or framework upgrades)', 'refactor*', 'migrat*', 'upgrade*'), kp('Faster understanding of code, logs, errors', 'understand*', 'logs', 'error*', 'debug*'), kp('Human review and quality gates remain', 'review', 'quality gate*', 'test*', 'verify')],
    'The biggest gains come from repetitive and exploratory work. I let the assistant scaffold new endpoints, DTOs, mappers and configuration that follow our existing patterns; generate unit tests, parameterised test cases and test data; perform mechanical refactorings and migrations, for example javax to jakarta for Spring Boot 3, or replacing deprecated APIs across many files; and write scripts, SQL and Dockerfiles. It also speeds up understanding: summarising an unfamiliar module, explaining a stack trace or log pattern, or comparing libraries. The time saved goes into design and review. All AI output still goes through code review, our test suite and quality gates, so speed does not cost quality.',
    'Can you give an example where AI saved you significant time?', sh),
  Q('nq-339', AI, 'E', 'concept', 'What is prompt engineering?',
    [kp('Designing inputs/instructions to get reliable outputs from an LLM', 'instructions', 'input*', 'llm', 'reliable'), kp('Clear task, context, constraints and output format', 'context', 'constraint*', 'format', 'clear'), kp('Examples (few-shot), roles/system prompts', 'few shot', 'few-shot', 'example*', 'system prompt', 'role'), kp('Step-by-step reasoning, breaking tasks down; iterating and evaluating', 'step by step', 'break* down', 'iterat*', 'evaluat*'), kp('Guard against hallucination and prompt injection', 'hallucinat*', 'prompt injection', 'ground*')],
    'Prompt engineering is the practice of designing the instructions and context given to a large language model so it produces accurate, useful and consistently formatted results. Good prompts state the task clearly, provide relevant context such as code, requirements or documents, define constraints like the Java version, coding standards or what not to change, and specify the output format, for example JSON or a unified diff. Techniques include giving examples, few-shot prompting, assigning a role in a system prompt, asking the model to reason step by step or splitting a big task into smaller prompts, and grounding answers in provided sources to reduce hallucinations. In applications it also means iterating with evaluation sets and defending against prompt injection from untrusted input.',
    'How would you get an LLM to return reliably parseable JSON?', sh),
  Q('nq-340', AI, 'E', 'scenario', 'How can Generative AI help with technical documentation?',
    [kp('Drafting docs from code: README, API docs, Javadoc', 'readme', 'api doc*', 'javadoc', 'draft*'), kp('Summarising changes: release notes, PR descriptions, ADR drafts', 'release notes', 'pull request', 'summari*', 'adr'), kp('Diagrams and runbooks from descriptions (Mermaid)', 'diagram*', 'mermaid', 'runbook*'), kp('Keeping docs in sync; consistency and clarity edits', 'in sync', 'up to date', 'consisten*', 'clarity'), kp('Human review for accuracy', 'review', 'accura*', 'verify')],
    'It removes the blank page problem. An AI tool can draft a README from the codebase, describing setup, configuration and endpoints; generate or improve Javadoc and OpenAPI descriptions; write pull request descriptions and release notes from commit diffs; draft architecture decision records from a discussion; produce sequence or architecture diagrams as Mermaid or PlantUML from a description; turn incident notes into runbooks; and rewrite existing docs for clarity and consistent terminology, or translate them. It can also flag docs that no longer match the code. A person who knows the system must still review everything for accuracy, since the model can invent behaviour.',
    'How would you keep AI-generated docs from going stale?', sh),
  Q('nq-341', AI, 'M', 'scenario', 'How can AI help create prototypes before implementing a business feature?',
    [kp('Quickly generate UI mockups or clickable prototypes', 'mockup*', 'prototype*', 'ui', 'clickable'), kp('Throwaway backend/API stubs and sample data', 'stub*', 'sample data', 'api', 'mock'), kp('Validate with stakeholders early; explore alternatives', 'stakeholder*', 'feedback', 'early', 'alternative*'), kp('Clarify requirements and edge cases', 'requirement*', 'edge case*', 'clarif*'), kp('Prototype is not production code', 'throwaway', 'not production', 'rewrite')],
    'Generative AI makes prototypes cheap. From a short description of the feature you can generate a clickable UI mockup or a simple web page, an OpenAPI draft of the endpoints, a stub backend returning realistic sample data, and even a working spike in a day instead of a sprint. Product owners and users can then try it and give feedback before the team invests in the real implementation, and you can compare two or three alternative flows. Building the prototype also forces edge cases and open questions to the surface, which improves the stories. The prototype should be treated as throwaway; production code is then written properly, with tests, security and our standards.',
    'How would you prevent a prototype from accidentally becoming production code?', sh),
  Q('nq-342', AI, 'M', 'scenario', 'How can AI help interpret business requirements in BRDs?',
    [kp('Summarise long BRDs and extract key requirements', 'summari*', 'extract*', 'key requirement*'), kp('Turn requirements into user stories with acceptance criteria', 'user stor*', 'acceptance criteria', 'gherkin'), kp('Find gaps, ambiguities and contradictions; generate questions', 'gap*', 'ambigu*', 'contradiction*', 'question*'), kp('Map to systems/data and draft test cases', 'test case*', 'data', 'system*', 'impact'), kp('Validate with business analysts; confidentiality', 'business analyst*', 'validate', 'confidential*')],
    'A business requirements document is often long and inconsistent. An LLM can summarise it, list the functional and non-functional requirements, and draft user stories with acceptance criteria in Given-When-Then form. It is especially useful for spotting gaps and contradictions, such as a rule mentioned in section 2 that conflicts with section 7, or undefined terms, missing error cases and unclear ownership, and for generating a list of clarification questions for the business analyst. It can also suggest which systems and data entities are affected and draft test scenarios. The output is a starting point that must be validated with the business, and the document should only go to tools approved for confidential data.',
    'What would you do if the AI summary and the BRD disagree?', sh),
  Q('nq-343', AI, 'M', 'scenario', 'Have you automated development activities using Generative AI?',
    [kp('Concrete automations: PR review bots, test generation, release notes', 'review bot', 'pull request', 'test generation', 'release notes'), kp('Integrated into CI/CD or scripts via LLM APIs', 'ci', 'pipeline', 'api', 'script*'), kp('Agentic tools performing multi-step tasks', 'agent*', 'multi step', 'multi-step'), kp('Guardrails: human approval, limited permissions, cost and quality checks', 'human', 'approval', 'permission*', 'cost', 'guardrail*'), kp('Results and lessons', 'result*', 'lesson*', 'saved', 'usefulness', 'tracked')],
    'Answer with what you built. Examples: a pipeline step that calls an LLM API to generate a summary and risk notes for each pull request and posts it as a comment; generating draft release notes from merged PRs; a script that proposes unit tests for uncovered classes, opened as a draft PR for review; using an agentic tool to upgrade dependencies across services, run the build and fix compile errors; and triaging failed builds by summarising logs. Explain the guardrails: AI suggestions never merge automatically, tokens have limited permissions, sensitive data is excluded, and you tracked cost and usefulness, for example the percentage of suggestions accepted.',
    'How did you measure whether the automation was worth it?', sh),
  Q('nq-344', AI, 'M', 'scenario', 'How can AI tools assist with code reviews?',
    [kp('Automated first-pass review: bugs, style, security issues', 'first pass', 'bug*', 'style', 'security'), kp('Summarise PR changes for reviewers', 'summari*', 'pull request', 'reviewer*'), kp('Suggest tests and edge cases, explain complex diffs', 'test*', 'edge case*', 'explain*'), kp('Human reviewer still approves; watch false positives', 'human', 'approve*', 'false positive*'), kp('Complement, not replace, static analysis', 'static analysis', 'sonarqube', 'complement')],
    'AI can act as a first reviewer on every pull request: summarising what changed and why for human reviewers, flagging likely bugs such as missing null checks, resource leaks, wrong transaction boundaries or N plus one queries, pointing out security issues like SQL injection or logging of personal data, checking naming and style against our conventions, and suggesting missing tests and edge cases. That lets human reviewers focus on design, domain logic and maintainability. It complements deterministic tools such as SonarQube, it does not replace them, and its comments can be wrong or noisy, so a human still decides and approves, and we tune it to reduce false positives.',
    'What kinds of issues do AI reviewers typically miss?', sh),
  Q('nq-345', AI, 'M', 'scenario', 'How can coding standards be provided to AI tools to improve automated code reviews?',
    [kp('Write standards in a repository instructions/rules file (e.g. CLAUDE.md, .cursor/rules, copilot-instructions.md)', 'claude.md', 'cursor', 'rules', 'copilot-instructions', 'instructions file'), kp('Include concrete examples of good/bad code and checklists', 'example*', 'checklist', 'good', 'bad'), kp('Reference architecture decisions, linters configs, security rules', 'adr', 'linter*', 'checkstyle', 'security'), kp('Provide relevant context in the prompt/RAG over docs', 'context', 'rag', 'retriev*', 'documentation'), kp('Version and refine the rules from feedback', 'version*', 'refine', 'feedback', 'iterat*')],
    'Put the standards where the tools can read them, versioned with the code: repository instruction files such as CLAUDE.md for Claude Code, .github/copilot-instructions.md for Copilot or Cursor project rules, describing conventions like package structure, constructor injection, error handling, logging rules, test naming and what is forbidden. Make them concrete, with short good and bad examples and a review checklist, and link to architecture decision records, Checkstyle and Sonar configurations and security guidelines. For a review bot built on an LLM API, include these rules in the system prompt and retrieve relevant docs, retrieval-augmented generation, for the changed files. Then refine the rules when the AI makes the same wrong comment twice, just like updating a human onboarding guide.',
    'Why keep these rules in the repository rather than in each developer\'s settings?', sh),
  Q('nq-346', AI, 'M', 'scenario', 'Have you integrated AI into a company workflow, or used it primarily as a development assistant?',
    [kp('Be clear which: personal assistant vs product/workflow integration', 'assistant', 'integrat*', 'workflow', 'product'), kp('Integration examples: chatbot, RAG on docs, ticket triage, document processing', 'chatbot', 'rag', 'triage', 'document processing', 'classification'), kp('Architecture: LLM API, embeddings/vector DB, Spring AI/LangChain4j', 'embedding*', 'vector', 'spring ai', 'langchain4j', 'api'), kp('Governance: privacy, evaluation, cost, human in the loop', 'privacy', 'evaluat*', 'cost', 'human in the loop', 'governance'), kp('Outcome/metrics', 'outcome', 'metric*', 'result*')],
    'Be honest about which applies. If mainly as an assistant, describe how it improved your daily work and that you understand how integration would work. If integrated: for example we built an internal support assistant that answers employees questions from Confluence and runbooks using retrieval-augmented generation: documents chunked and embedded into a vector store, relevant chunks retrieved per question and sent to the LLM through Spring AI, with citations. Or ticket triage that classifies incoming issues and routes them, with a human confirming. Mention governance: approved providers, no personal data, prompt injection protection, evaluation sets to measure answer quality, cost monitoring, and the measured outcome, such as fewer escalations.',
    'How would you evaluate the quality of a RAG system?', sh),
  Q('nq-347', AI, 'M', 'concept', 'What is Model Context Protocol (MCP)?',
    [kp('Open standard (introduced by Anthropic, 2024) connecting AI apps to tools and data', 'open standard', 'open protocol', 'anthropic', 'tools', 'data'), kp('Client-server architecture over JSON-RPC', 'json-rpc', 'json rpc', 'client', 'server'), kp('Servers expose tools, resources and prompts', 'tools', 'resources', 'prompts'), kp('One integration usable by many AI hosts (like USB-C for AI)', 'usb-c', 'usb c', 'reuse', 'any host', 'many'), kp('Transports: stdio and streamable HTTP', 'stdio', 'http', 'transport*')],
    'The Model Context Protocol is an open standard, introduced by Anthropic in late 2024 and now widely adopted, for connecting AI applications to external tools and data sources. It is often described as USB-C for AI: instead of every AI app writing custom integrations for every system, a system exposes an MCP server once, and any MCP-compatible host, such as Claude, an IDE assistant or your own agent, can use it. It uses a client-server architecture with JSON-RPC 2.0 messages over transports like stdio for local servers or streamable HTTP for remote ones. Servers expose three main kinds of capabilities: tools the model can call, resources it can read, and reusable prompt templates.',
    'How is MCP different from simply calling a REST API from an LLM app?', sh),
  Q('nq-348', AI, 'M', 'concept', 'Why is MCP used to connect AI applications with external tools?',
    [kp('Standardisation: avoids N x M custom integrations', 'standard*', 'n x m', 'n*m', 'custom integration*'), kp('Reusable servers across hosts and models', 'reus*', 'any host', 'model agnostic', 'portable'), kp('Gives models live, up-to-date context and the ability to act', 'context', 'up to date', 'live', 'act*'), kp('Discovery of capabilities, consistent security/permission model', 'discover*', 'permission*', 'security', 'consent'), kp('Ecosystem of existing servers (GitHub, databases, Slack)', 'ecosystem', 'github', 'database*', 'slack')],
    'Without a standard, each AI application needs its own integration with each tool, an N times M problem, and every integration has different formats and security handling. MCP standardises this: a tool provider writes one MCP server, and any compliant host and model can discover and use its tools, resources and prompts at runtime, so integrations are reusable and portable across vendors. It gives models access to live, up-to-date context, like tickets, code or database records, and the ability to take actions, which turns a chat model into a useful agent. It also provides a consistent place to apply permissions, user consent and auditing, and there is a growing ecosystem of ready-made servers for GitHub, databases, file systems and SaaS tools.',
    'What security risks come with connecting tools to an LLM?', sh),
  Q('nq-349', AI, 'M', 'concept', 'What are the main components of MCP architecture?',
    [kp('Host: the AI application the user interacts with', 'host'), kp('Client: connector inside the host, one per server connection', 'client', 'one per server', 'connection'), kp('Server: exposes tools, resources, prompts', 'server', 'tools', 'resources', 'prompts'), kp('Protocol layer JSON-RPC; transports stdio/HTTP', 'json-rpc', 'json rpc', 'stdio', 'http'), kp('Capability negotiation at initialisation; client features like sampling/roots', 'initiali*', 'capabilit*', 'sampling', 'roots', 'elicitation')],
    'MCP has three roles. The host is the AI application the user works with, such as Claude Desktop, an IDE or a custom agent, which manages the model and user consent. Inside it, an MCP client maintains a one-to-one connection with one MCP server, so a host connecting to GitHub and a database has two clients. An MCP server is a program that exposes capabilities: tools, resources and prompts. Underneath is the protocol layer: JSON-RPC 2.0 messages for requests, responses and notifications, carried over a transport, stdio for local servers or streamable HTTP for remote ones. Connections start with an initialisation handshake negotiating protocol version and capabilities; clients can also offer features to servers, like sampling, roots and elicitation.',
    'Why does the host manage user consent rather than the server?', sh),
  Q('nq-350', AI, 'M', 'concept', 'What are an MCP host, client, and server?',
    [kp('Host: user-facing AI app that runs the model and coordinates clients', 'host', 'user facing', 'ai app*', 'coordinat*'), kp('Client: protocol connector created by the host for one server', 'client', 'one server', 'connector'), kp('Server: lightweight program exposing tools/resources/prompts for a system', 'server', 'expos*', 'tools'), kp('Local (stdio) or remote (HTTP) servers', 'local', 'remote', 'stdio', 'http'), kp('Example mapping', 'example', 'claude desktop', 'ide', 'github')],
    'The host is the AI application the user interacts with: for example Claude Desktop, an IDE assistant or your own chatbot. It runs or calls the language model, decides which servers are connected, and enforces user permissions. The client lives inside the host; the host creates one client per server, and it speaks the MCP protocol to that server, handling initialisation, listing tools and sending calls. The server is a separate program that wraps a system, such as GitHub, a PostgreSQL database or a company API, and exposes tools, resources and prompts in MCP format. It can run locally as a subprocess over stdio or remotely over HTTP. So: Claude Desktop is a host, it creates a client for the GitHub MCP server, and the model uses the server create_issue tool.',
    'Could a single application be both an MCP client and server?', sh),
  Q('nq-351', AI, 'SL', 'concept', 'How does an AI model use MCP to access external tools or information?',
    [kp('Client lists server tools (tools/list) with names, descriptions and JSON schemas', 'tools/list', 'schema*', 'description*', 'list'), kp('Host passes tool definitions to the model', 'tool definition*', 'pass*', 'model'), kp('Model decides to call a tool, producing arguments', 'decide*', 'tool call', 'arguments'), kp('Host/client sends tools/call to the server (after user approval), returns result to the model', 'tools/call', 'approv*', 'result'), kp('Resources read via resources/read for context', 'resources/read', 'resource*')],
    'At connection time the client asks the server for its capabilities, for example tools/list returns each tool name, a description and a JSON Schema of its inputs. The host includes these tool definitions in the context it sends to the model. When the user asks something, the model decides whether a tool would help and responds with a structured tool call, a name and arguments matching the schema. The host checks policy and, where required, asks the user to approve, then the client sends a tools/call request to the server, which executes it, for example running a query, and returns the result content. The host feeds the result back to the model, which may call further tools or answer the user. Resources work similarly through resources/list and resources/read to load data into context.',
    'Why do tool descriptions matter so much for the model?', sh),
  Q('nq-352', AI, 'SL', 'concept', 'How does an MCP server expose tools, prompts, and capabilities?',
    [kp('Declares capabilities during initialize handshake', 'initialize', 'initialise', 'handshake', 'capabilit*'), kp('Tools: name, description, inputSchema; handles tools/call', 'inputschema', 'tools/call', 'name', 'description'), kp('Resources: URIs with content, list/read/subscribe', 'resource*', 'uri', 'subscribe'), kp('Prompts: reusable templates with arguments via prompts/get', 'prompts/get', 'template*', 'prompt*'), kp('SDKs (TypeScript, Python, Java/Spring AI) and change notifications', 'sdk', 'spring ai', 'python', 'typescript', 'notification*', 'list_changed')],
    'During the initialize handshake the server declares which capabilities it supports, such as tools, resources, prompts and logging, and whether it sends change notifications. Tools are listed with a unique name, a human-readable description and an inputSchema in JSON Schema, optionally an output schema and annotations like read-only or destructive; the server implements a handler for tools/call that validates arguments and returns content and errors. Resources are identified by URIs, such as file or database URIs, and can be listed, read and subscribed to. Prompts are reusable message templates with arguments, retrieved with prompts/get, often exposed as slash commands. Servers are usually written with official SDKs, TypeScript, Python, Java, including Spring AI MCP, which generate the protocol plumbing from annotated functions.',
    'How would you expose an existing Spring service as MCP tools?', sh),
  Q('nq-353', AI, 'SL', 'design', 'How can permissions and approved operations be controlled when an AI application uses external tools?',
    [kp('Least privilege: scoped credentials/tokens per server, read-only where possible', 'least privilege', 'scoped', 'read only', 'read-only', 'token*'), kp('Human-in-the-loop approval for sensitive or destructive actions', 'human in the loop', 'approval', 'confirm*', 'destructive'), kp('Allow-lists of tools/operations and policies enforced by host or gateway', 'allow list', 'allowlist', 'polic*', 'gateway'), kp('Authentication (OAuth) for remote MCP servers; per-user identity', 'oauth', 'authenticat*', 'per user'), kp('Audit logging, rate limits, sandboxing; defend against prompt injection', 'audit', 'rate limit*', 'sandbox*', 'prompt injection')],
    'Treat the model as untrusted and put controls around its tools. Give each MCP server or tool the least privilege it needs: scoped, short-lived credentials, read-only database users where possible, and act with the end user identity, using OAuth for remote servers, so the AI can never do more than the user. In the host, configure allow-lists of which servers and tools are enabled, and require explicit human approval for sensitive or destructive operations such as deleting data, sending emails, payments or production changes, while read-only actions can be pre-approved. Add policy checks in a gateway, rate limits and spending limits, sandbox local execution, log every tool call for audit, and defend against prompt injection, for example content from a web page or ticket telling the model to exfiltrate data, by validating inputs and never letting retrieved content grant permissions.',
    'How would a prompt injection attack exploit an over-privileged tool?', sh),

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 25. Project experience and introduction (Java)
  Q('nq-354', PX, 'EMSL', 'scenario', 'Explain your current project and your responsibilities.',
    [kp('Business context: domain, users, scale', 'domain', 'business', 'users', 'scale', 'customers'), kp('Architecture and tech stack', 'architecture', 'microservice*', 'spring boot', 'kafka', 'tech stack'), kp('Your specific responsibilities and ownership', 'responsib*', 'own*', 'my role'), kp('Concrete achievement with impact/metrics', 'improv*', 'reduc*', 'impact', 'metric*', 'percent'), kp('Team and process', 'team', 'agile', 'scrum')],
    'Use a short structure: business context, architecture, your role, one or two achievements. For example: I work on an order management platform for a retail company handling about fifty thousand orders a day. It is a set of Spring Boot microservices on Kubernetes, with PostgreSQL, Kafka for events and Redis caching, behind an API gateway. I am a senior developer in a team of eight; I own the order and payment services: designing APIs, implementing features, reviewing code, improving performance and supporting production. A recent achievement: I redesigned payment status handling with the outbox pattern and idempotent consumers, which removed duplicate charges and reduced stuck orders by ninety percent. Keep it to about two minutes and be ready to go deeper.',
    'What was the most challenging technical problem in this project?', src),
  Q('nq-355', PX, 'EMS', 'scenario', 'Which Java, Spring Boot, Hibernate, and microservices technologies have you used in your projects?',
    [kp('Java version and language features', 'java 17', 'java 21', 'java 11', 'stream*', 'record*'), kp('Spring Boot modules: Web, Data JPA, Security, Actuator, Cloud', 'spring web', 'data jpa', 'spring security', 'actuator', 'spring cloud'), kp('Hibernate/JPA specifics: mappings, fetch strategies, N+1, caching, Flyway/Liquibase', 'hibernate', 'lazy', 'n+1', 'flyway', 'liquibase', 'entity graph'), kp('Microservices stack: gateway, discovery/config, Kafka, Resilience4j, Docker/K8s', 'gateway', 'eureka', 'config server', 'kafka', 'resilience4j', 'kubernetes'), kp('Testing and observability tools', 'junit', 'testcontainers', 'prometheus', 'grafana', 'tracing')],
    'Give a structured list tied to real usage. Java: currently 17 or 21, using streams, records, Optional, CompletableFuture and virtual threads where useful. Spring Boot 3: Spring Web for REST APIs with validation, Spring Data JPA, Spring Security as an OAuth2 resource server with JWT, Actuator with Micrometer, scheduling and caching. Hibernate: entity mappings and relationships, lazy loading and solving N plus one problems with fetch joins and entity graphs, optimistic locking with @Version, batch inserts, and Flyway for migrations. Microservices: Spring Cloud Gateway, config through Kubernetes ConfigMaps, Kafka with Spring Kafka, Resilience4j circuit breakers and retries, OpenFeign or WebClient, Docker and Kubernetes with Helm. Testing with JUnit 5, Mockito and Testcontainers; observability with Prometheus, Grafana and OpenTelemetry tracing.',
    'Can you tell me about an N+1 problem you found and how you fixed it?', src),
  Q('nq-356', PX, 'MS', 'scenario', 'What is your experience with CI/CD pipelines?',
    [kp('Tools used (Jenkins, GitHub Actions, GitLab, Azure DevOps)', 'jenkins', 'github actions', 'gitlab', 'azure devops'), kp('Pipeline stages you built or maintained', 'stage*', 'build', 'test', 'scan', 'deploy'), kp('Deployment approach (Helm, Argo CD, environments, approvals)', 'helm', 'argo*', 'environment*', 'approval*'), kp('Improvements: speed, reliability, quality gates', 'improv*', 'faster', 'quality gate', 'reduced'), kp('Ownership level (wrote vs used)', 'wrote', 'set up', 'maintain*', 'owned')],
    'Describe what you used and what you personally built. For example: our services use Jenkins declarative pipelines from a shared library: build with Maven, unit and Testcontainers integration tests run in parallel, SonarQube quality gate, Snyk dependency scan, Docker image build with Jib and Trivy scan, push to ECR, then deployment through Helm, automatically to dev and QA, and with an approval step for staging and production, followed by smoke tests. I wrote the pipeline stages for my services, added caching of Maven dependencies and parallel tests which cut build time from twenty-five to nine minutes, and introduced automatic rollback when smoke tests fail. Be clear whether you designed the pipeline or mainly used one maintained by a platform team.',
    'How did you reduce pipeline time or make it more reliable?', src),
  Q('nq-357', PX, 'SL', 'design', 'How have you migrated, or how would you migrate, a monolithic application to microservices?',
    [kp('Assess and define boundaries with DDD/bounded contexts', 'bounded context*', 'domain', 'ddd', 'boundar*'), kp('Incremental Strangler Fig with routing layer, not big bang', 'strangler', 'incremental', 'big bang', 'gateway', 'routing'), kp('Data decomposition: split database, CDC/sync, anti-corruption layer', 'database', 'data', 'cdc', 'anti corruption', 'anti-corruption'), kp('Platform readiness: CI/CD, observability, containers, resilience', 'ci/cd', 'observability', 'monitor*', 'kubernetes', 'resilience'), kp('Prioritise by value/risk; measure; team organisation', 'priorit*', 'value', 'risk', 'team*', 'valuable', 'measuring')],
    'I would not do a big-bang rewrite. First understand the domain and current pain points, and identify bounded contexts with domain-driven design and event storming, using code and data coupling analysis, so service boundaries follow business capabilities. In parallel, prepare the platform: CI/CD, containers and Kubernetes, centralised logging, metrics and tracing, and an API gateway. Then apply the Strangler Fig pattern: choose a first capability that is valuable but loosely coupled, such as notifications or catalogue, build it as a service, route its traffic through the gateway and remove it from the monolith. Data is the hard part: give the new service its own schema, sync with change data capture during transition, and use an anti-corruption layer and events instead of shared tables. Repeat, measuring deployment frequency and incidents, and keep the monolith modular meanwhile.',
    'How would you split a shared database during migration?', src),
];
